WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.454 --> 00:00:04.551
- The best way to explore a
new world is to land on it.

00:00:04.551 --> 00:00:06.604
That's why humans have sent spacecraft

00:00:06.604 --> 00:00:11.365
to the Moon, Venus, Mars,
Saturn's moon Titan, and more.

00:00:11.365 --> 00:00:13.505
But there are a few
places in the solar system

00:00:13.505 --> 00:00:16.736
we will never understand
as well as we'd like.

00:00:16.736 --> 00:00:19.027
One of them is Jupiter.

00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:22.465
Jupiter is made of mostly
hydrogen and helium gas,

00:00:22.465 --> 00:00:24.227
so trying to land on
it would be like trying

00:00:24.227 --> 00:00:26.539
to land on a cloud here on Earth.

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:29.453
There's no outer crust to
break your fall on Jupiter,

00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:32.381
just an endless stretch of atmosphere.

00:00:32.381 --> 00:00:34.034
The big question then is,

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:38.003
could you fall through one end
of Jupiter and out the other?

00:00:38.003 --> 00:00:40.903
Turns out, you wouldn't
even make it halfway.

00:00:40.903 --> 00:00:44.320
(mysterious synth music)

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.638
First thing's first, Jupiter's
atmosphere has no oxygen,

00:00:49.638 --> 00:00:52.848
so make sure you bring
plenty with you to breathe.

00:00:52.848 --> 00:00:55.846
The next problem is the
scorching temperatures,

00:00:55.846 --> 00:00:57.706
so pack an air conditioner.

00:00:57.706 --> 00:01:02.106
Now you're ready for a
journey of epic proportions.

00:01:02.106 --> 00:01:03.976
For scale, here's how many Earths

00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:07.537
you could stack from Jupiter's center.

00:01:07.537 --> 00:01:09.207
As you enter the top of the atmosphere,

00:01:09.207 --> 00:01:12.871
you're traveling at 110,000 miles-per-hour

00:01:12.871 --> 00:01:15.314
under the pull of Jupiter's gravity.

00:01:15.314 --> 00:01:16.704
But brace yourself.

00:01:16.704 --> 00:01:18.992
You'll quickly hit the
denser atmosphere below,

00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:21.115
which will hit you like a wall.

00:01:21.115 --> 00:01:23.014
It won't be enough to stop you, though.

00:01:23.014 --> 00:01:24.082
After about three minutes,

00:01:24.082 --> 00:01:28.149
you'll reach the cloud
tops 155 miles down.

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:30.359
Here, you'll experience the full brunt

00:01:30.359 --> 00:01:32.426
of Jupiter's rotation.

00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:35.027
Jupiter is actually the
fastest rotating planet

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:36.441
in our solar system.

00:01:36.441 --> 00:01:40.038
One day lasts about
nine-and-a-half Earth hours.

00:01:40.038 --> 00:01:43.010
This creates powerful winds
that can whip around the planet

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.141
at more than 300 miles-per-hour.

00:01:46.141 --> 00:01:48.357
About 75 miles below the clouds,

00:01:48.357 --> 00:01:51.454
you reach the limit of human exploration.

00:01:51.454 --> 00:01:53.121
Galileo probe made it this far

00:01:53.121 --> 00:01:56.637
when it dove into Jupiter's
atmosphere in 1995.

00:01:56.637 --> 00:02:00.002
It only lasted 58 minutes
before losing contact

00:02:00.002 --> 00:02:03.510
and was eventually destroyed
by the crushing pressure.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.967
Down here, the pressure is
nearly 100 times what it is

00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:11.209
on Earth's surface and you
won't be able to see anything,

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:12.883
so you'll have to rely on instruments

00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:15.863
to explore your surroundings.

00:02:15.863 --> 00:02:20.363
By 430 miles down, the
pressure is 1,150 times higher.

00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:23.891
You might be able to survive down here

00:02:23.891 --> 00:02:25.558
if you were in a spacecraft built like

00:02:25.558 --> 00:02:27.272
the Trieste submarine,

00:02:27.272 --> 00:02:30.563
the deepest-diving submarine on Earth.

00:02:30.563 --> 00:02:32.773
Any deeper and the pressure
and the temperature

00:02:32.773 --> 00:02:35.942
will be too great for
the spacecraft to endure.

00:02:35.942 --> 00:02:39.007
However, let's say that
you could find a way

00:02:39.007 --> 00:02:41.283
to descend even further.

00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:45.035
You will uncover some of
Jupiter's grandest mysteries.

00:02:45.035 --> 00:02:48.309
But sadly, you'll have
no way to tell anyone.

00:02:48.309 --> 00:02:52.048
Jupiter's deep atmosphere
absorbs radio waves,

00:02:52.048 --> 00:02:54.398
so you'd be shut off
from the outside world,

00:02:54.398 --> 00:02:56.248
unable to communicate.

00:02:56.248 --> 00:02:58.907
Once you've reached 2,500 miles down,

00:02:58.907 --> 00:03:02.574
the temperature is 6,100
degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:05.846
That's hot enough to melt tungsten,

00:03:05.846 --> 00:03:09.908
the metal with the highest
melting point in the universe.

00:03:09.908 --> 00:03:11.873
At this point, you will have been falling

00:03:11.873 --> 00:03:16.662
for at least 12 hours and you
won't even be halfway through.

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:18.714
At 13,000 miles down,

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:21.570
you'll reach Jupiter's innermost layer.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.404
Here, the pressure is two
million times stronger

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:26.721
than that of Earth's surface

00:03:26.721 --> 00:03:31.136
and the temperature is hotter
than the surface of the Sun.

00:03:31.136 --> 00:03:33.449
These conditions are so
extreme that they change

00:03:33.449 --> 00:03:36.294
the chemistry of hydrogen around you.

00:03:36.294 --> 00:03:39.035
Hydrogen molecules are
forced so close together

00:03:39.035 --> 00:03:41.126
that their electrons break loose,

00:03:41.126 --> 00:03:45.160
forming an unusual substance
called metallic hydrogen.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.578
Metallic hydrogen is highly reflective,

00:03:47.578 --> 00:03:50.166
so if you tried using
lights to see down there,

00:03:50.166 --> 00:03:53.741
it would be impossible, and
it's as dense as a rock.

00:03:53.741 --> 00:03:55.416
So as you travel deeper,

00:03:55.416 --> 00:03:57.512
the buoyancy force from
the metallic hydrogen

00:03:57.512 --> 00:04:00.400
counteracts gravity's downward pull.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.546
Eventually, that buoyancy
will shoot you back up

00:04:03.546 --> 00:04:06.049
until gravity pulls you right back down,

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:10.088
sort of like a yo-yo, and
when those two forces equal,

00:04:10.088 --> 00:04:12.959
you'll be left free-floating
in mid-Jupiter,

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:16.876
unable to move up or down
and no way of escape.

00:04:17.878 --> 00:04:22.347
Suffice it to say, trying to
land on Jupiter is a bad idea.

00:04:22.347 --> 00:04:26.492
We may never see what's
beneath those majestic clouds.

00:04:26.492 --> 00:04:29.074
But we can still study and admire

00:04:29.074 --> 00:04:31.824
this mysterious planet from afar.

